int two_sum(const int *nums, int n, int target, int *out) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (nums[i] + nums[j] == target) {
                out[0] = i;
                out[1] = j;
                return 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Store each value's index in a hash map as you scan. For each number, check whether target minus that number has already been seen. C has no built-in array length or string type, so the length is passed explicitly or the string is NUL-terminated.

**Complexity:** O(n) time, O(n) space using a hash map (the brute-force double loop is O(n²)).

For example, `two_sum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9)` gives `0 1`.

